>Symbios 53c810 controller chip. I am using these settings in
>the kernel config
>CONFIG_SCSI_SYM53C8XX_2=3Dy
>CONFIG_SCSI_SYM53C8XX_DMA_ADDRESSING_MODE=3D0
>CONFIG_SCSI_SYM53C8XX_DEFAULT_TAGS=3D16
>CONFIG_SCSI_SYM53C8XX_MAX_TAGS=3D16
># CONFIG_SCSI_SYM53C8XX_IOMAPPED is not set
>(I tried using IOMAPPED and different DMA modes and number of TAGs
>also, but the message still shows up)
>
>in 2.4.30, the driver version is sym-2.1.17a and it works fine
>(without any of these REJECT messages), while 2.6.12.5, the
>driver version is 2.2.x I believe.
>
>does anyone on the list know of an issue which could cause this ?
>is the REJECT message a cause for concern ? (system seem to work
>fine as before)
> =20
>
I have this problem with my SYM53C810 controller too, and the only=20
solution I've found (that worked) was to manually modify the kernel's=20
SCSI driver. See this page for the details :
http://lists.debian.org/debian-alpha/2005/05/msg00041.html

 From what I understand (which may well be a simplistic understanding of=20
the real situation), the problem is that the drive connected to the=20
controller is FAST-WIDE SCSI 2, but the controller can't do WIDE. The=20
driver tries to set it to WIDE, then send some data to see if it works,=20
and should fail (the second if in the code in the above page) but=20
doesn't, so the controller is left in WIDE mode even though it doesn't=20
support it and that's why it gives those messages. The mystery is why=20
the WIDE mode transfer doesn't fail as it should.

What I've done is comment out the whole section of code (starting with=20
the if right after the /* test width */ comment and ending with the=20
closing curly brace for that if) and then compile the kernel. That way=20
the driver never sets the controller to WIDE mode at all. Works great.=20
I'd really like someone to find a more sophisticated solution that=20
commenting out the whole lot, maybe fixing the test that should fail but=20
doesn't? Unfortunately my knowledge of Linux kernel drivers is too=20
limited to do that.

This modification affects the SCSI driver, though, which in my case=20
isn't a problem since I'm only using this controller, but if your=20
machine has another controller that should support WIDE mode, then this=20
change will probably affect it too... Watch out for that.

I wonder also why the patch YosWinK pointed to affects a totally=20
different source file... I'm not sure it's the same problem, because I'm=20
on the RedHat-Alpha mailing list too and haven't heard of this specific=20
problem. Perhaps it addresses another problem in the 53C810 code on Alpha.

Other than that minor problem, I've been running a 2.6 kernel for a=20
couple months now on my AlphaXL 366 with a 50GB SCSI-2 drive. Runs=20
great. Of course, it's a single processor machine so I can't help you=20
with the other problem you're having.

Hope this helps,

J-S

